{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on direction proposal 118c9930. The high-level choice is correct: choose one exclusive resident per repository, refuse a second live resident, take over only after a definitive negative probe, preserve the Git CAS path, and reject a shared Nexus or leader as unnecessary complexity. The proposed ownership primitive is not yet safe enough to implement. O_EXCL creates resident.json before a complete URL and identity can be written, while the phase-2 protocol says an unparseable record may be replaced. A simultaneous starter can observe that in-progress file as unparseable and replace it; two starters that both classify one stale record can also race through non-conditional remove or rename and both serve. The current temp-file rename is last-writer-wins, not compare-and-swap. Specify and test one atomic acquisition and stale-transfer protocol that has exactly one winner, never treats an incomplete or malformed claim as vacant, and closes a bound loser before it serves. A separate exclusive coordination file or directory is acceptable if it is the ownership authority and resident.json remains endpoint metadata; rejecting it merely because the two records can disagree does not solve the single-file transfer race. Reconcile the bind order for port zero explicitly: binding before ownership may be acceptable only if the listener never serves and is closed on loss. Probe every existing claim conservatively, including a different-genesis record, and make the identity-mismatch rule consistent; the proposal currently alternates between refusing and replacing. Tie withdrawal and any identity comparison to the claimed owner or expected generation, or remove the generation field if it is not compared. Architecture: option (a) preserves the kernel/fold CAS contract and changes ordinary Git storage, application-host lifecycle, Nexus/service composition, and the gs surface; the ownership-versus-advertisement contract must be documented in the same head. Security: the read-only loopback identity endpoint is acceptable because genesis and generation are not secrets, provided the probe reuses strict loopback URL validation, has bounded reads, exposes no credential, enables no CORS authority, and performs no side effect; malformed-claim replacement remains an unresolved integrity defect. Simplification: shared Nexus/leader election is fluff, but overloading advertisement as both lock and endpoint is not simpler when safe stale transfer needs a second coordination mechanism or a proven atomic state machine. Keep the owner nonce and fail-closed ambiguity; cut any returned identity field that is not stored and compared. Please revise the direction before phase 2. No implementation was reviewed or authorized.","body":{"proposal":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:118c993080f6f064879aa3ea2cd53a0cd6179013","verdict":"changes-requested"}}